<title>FRANCE: GM sees EMU boosting euro competitiveness.</title>
<headline>GM sees EMU boosting euro competitiveness.</headline>
<dateline>PARIS 1997-06-25</dateline>
<text>
<p>The introduction of a common currency in Europe on January 1, 1999 will make Europe a more competitive and dangerous commercial force in the world, General Motors Corp  Chairman John Smith said in a French newspaper interview.</p>
<p>&quot;Europe will be commercially stronger in the world with a single currency instead of 15 currencies. Europe will become a much more dangerous competitor,&quot; Smith said in an interview with daily Le Figaro.</p>
<p>He added that the dollar's rise against European currencies was not a problem for the United States but that the yen's level was important.</p>
<p>&quot;The change of the dollar's level compared to European currencies is not a problem for the United States, but on the other hand we follow the level of the yen closely because we have a big trade deficit with Japan,&quot; Smith told the paper.</p>
<p>-- Paris Newsroom +331 4221 5452</p>
